      "text": "✅ Major Active Volcanoes\n\n1. Kīlauea (Hawaii, USA)– World’s most active volcano, continuous eruptions since 2024; shield volcano type.\n\n2. Mount Etna (Sicily, Italy)– Europe’s most active volcano; frequent strombolian and effusive eruptions.\n \n3. Stromboli (Aeolian Islands, Italy)– Called “Lighthouse of the Mediterranean”; continuous mild eruptions since 1934. \n\n4. Mauna Loa (Hawaii, USA)– Largest active volcano on Earth; shield type with massive lava flows.\n\n5. Piton de la Fournaise (Réunion Island, France)– One of the world’s most frequently erupting volcanoes; effusive eruptions.\n\n6. Merapi (Java, Indonesia)– One of Asia’s most dangerous volcanoes; frequent explosive eruptions; near dense population.\n\n7. Reventador (Ecuador)– Active since 2008; continuous small explosive eruptions typical of Andean belt.\n\n8. Mount Erebus (Antarctica)– Southernmost active volcano; has a permanent lava lake.\n\n9. Nyamuragira (DR Congo)– Africa’s most active volcano; major effusive lava eruptions in Great Rift Valley.\n\n10. Mount Vesuvius (Italy)– Active but presently dormant; last major eruption in 1944, potential threat to Naples.\n\n11. **Mount Rainier (Washington, USA)** – Active stratovolcano; considered hazardous due to glaciers and lahars.\n\n12. **Semeru (Indonesia)** – Indonesia’s highest volcano; continuous plume activity since 2019.\n\n13. **Taal (Philippines)** – Caldera-type volcano; phreatomagmatic eruptions; near Manila region.\n\n14. **Aira (Sakurajima, Japan)** – Frequent explosive activity; near Kagoshima, part of Aira caldera system.\n\n15. **Sheveluch (Kamchatka, Russia)** – One of Russia’s largest and most explosive volcanoes.\n\n16. **Ulawun (Papua New Guinea)** – Pacific “Ring of Fire” volcano; erupted March 2025; highly risky zone.\n\n17. **Sabancaya (Peru)** – Active 2025; frequent ash plumes in Andes region.\n\n18. **Lewotolok (Indonesia)** – Active since 2023; frequent ash explosions; located in Lesser Sunda Islands.\n\n19. **Yasur (Vanuatu)** – Constant activity for 800+ years; strombolian eruptions visible nightly.\n\n20. **Erta Ale (Ethiopia)** – Active basaltic shield volcano in Afar Rift; famous for persi",
